 ## Operating characteristics
 
-*   **Time Complexity:** LOD selection is $O(B)$ where $B$ is the number of bricks. Hierarchy construction is $O(V)$ where $V$ is the total voxel count. Rendering is $O(P \cdot S)$ where $P$ is pixels and $S$ is samples, but $S$ is reduced by LOD. Clipping operations average 2500 per frame, up to 5500 in worst-case diagonal views [S3].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Texture memory usage is significantly reduced. For four levels of detail, memory usage drops to 29% for front-to-back rendering, or 38% for other configurations, compared to the original [S3].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. Clipping operations average 2500 per frame, up to 5500 in worst-case diagonal views [S3].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Texture memory usage is significantly reduced [S3]. For four levels of detail, memory usage drops to 29% for front-to-back rendering, or 38% for other configurations, compared to the original [S3].
 *   **Performance:** On an engine dataset, LOD reduced texture lookups to 32% (or 41% in another configuration) of original and frame time from ~2 seconds to 0.5 seconds [S2, S3]. Clipping overhead is <0.1 seconds, texture adaptation takes ~0.2 seconds [S3].
-*   **Strengths:** Interactive frame rates for large datasets; continuous interpolation; correct opacity accumulation.
+*   **Strengths:** Interactive frame rates for large datasets; continuous interpolation; correct opacity accumulation [S1, S2, S3].
 *   **Limitations:** Clipping overhead; power-of-two memory waste; potential topology distortion if LOD selection relies solely on focus point oracles without error measures [S3].
 
